Day 11 – Docker Networking & Container Communication

Objective

Understand Docker networking, network drivers, bridge networks, network creation and inspection, and container-to-container communication. Practice connecting containers to networks and troubleshooting network connectivity.

1. Docker Networking Fundamentals

Docker networking enables containers to communicate with each other, the host machine, and external networks.

In multi-container applications, different components such as frontend, backend, and database may run in separate containers. Docker networks provide connectivity between these components.

2. Docker Network Drivers

Learned the purpose of different network drivers:

- Bridge: Connects containers through a virtual network on a single Docker host.
- Host: Allows a container to share the host's network namespace.
- None: Provides no normal network connectivity to the container.
- Overlay: Enables networking across multiple Docker hosts in supported environments.

Bridge networking is especially useful for local multi-container applications.

3. Default Bridge vs User-Defined Bridge

Default Bridge

Docker automatically creates a default bridge network. Containers connected to it can communicate using IP addresses, but automatic container-name DNS resolution is not provided in the same way as user-defined bridge networks.

User-defined Bridge

A custom bridge network provides better control over container connectivity and automatic DNS resolution through container names.

Benefits:

- Container-to-container communication
- Name-based communication without manually tracking container IPs
- Better network organization and isolation
- Easier management of multi-container applications

4. Network Creation and Inspection

Created and inspected Docker networks using:

sudo docker network ls
sudo docker network inspect bridge
sudo docker network create app-network
sudo docker network inspect app-network

Learned to inspect network properties such as:

- Network ID and name
- Driver and scope
- Subnet and gateway
- Connected containers
- IPAM configuration

Subnet defines the IP address range available to the Docker network, while the gateway provides a routing point for network traffic.

5. Connecting Containers to Networks

Created an Nginx container and connected it to a user-defined bridge network:

sudo docker run -d --name test-nginx --network app-network nginx

Verified the container's network attachment using:

sudo docker network inspect app-network
sudo docker inspect test-nginx

Also learned how to connect an existing running container to a network without recreating it:

sudo docker network connect app-network troubleshoot-net

Verified the network attachment by inspecting the network configuration.

6. Container-to-Container Communication

Created two Nginx containers on the same user-defined bridge network:

sudo docker network create app-network

sudo docker run -d --name container1 --network app-network nginx

sudo docker run -d --name container2 --network app-network nginx

Entered the first container:

sudo docker exec -it container1 bash

Checked whether curl was installed:

curl --version

Sent an HTTP request from "container1" to "container2":

curl http://container2

Received the default Nginx HTML response successfully.

This verified that containers connected to the same user-defined bridge network can communicate using container names. Docker's internal DNS resolves the container name to its network IP, allowing communication without manually specifying the IP address.

7. Bash and Curl

Reviewed the difference between Bash and curl:

- Bash: A shell used to execute Linux commands and automate tasks through scripts.
- Bash scripting: Writing multiple commands in a script file to automate operations.
- curl: A command-line tool used to send HTTP/HTTPS requests and receive responses from servers.

Used curl to verify HTTP communication between Docker containers.

8. Network Troubleshooting

Scenario 1 – Container Not Connected to the Required Network

Created a container without specifying a custom network:

sudo docker run -d --name troubleshoot-net nginx

Connected the running container to the existing network without recreating it:

sudo docker network connect app-network troubleshoot-net

Verified the connection using:

sudo docker network inspect app-network

Successfully attached the running container to the network and confirmed its presence in the network inspection output.

Key Learnings

- Docker network drivers control how container networking operates.
- Bridge networking is useful for containers running on the same Docker host.
- User-defined bridge networks support automatic DNS resolution between containers.
- Subnets and gateways are part of Docker's internal network configuration.
- Containers can communicate using container names instead of manually tracking IP addresses.
- "docker network inspect" helps investigate network configuration and attached containers.
- "docker network connect" allows an existing running container to join another network without recreating it.
- "curl" can be used to test HTTP connectivity and verify server responses.

